To solve this, we first notice the AM-GM inequality can be applied to each of the three terms in the product. We want to minimize \((x + 2y)(y + 2z)(xz + 1)\) under the constraint \(xyz = 1\). By AM-GM: 
\begin{align*}
x + 2y &\geq 2\sqrt{2xy} \\
y + 2z &\geq 2\sqrt{2yz} \\
xz + 1 &\geq 2\sqrt{xz}
\end{align*}
Multiplying these inequalities, we get:
\[(x + 2y)(y + 2z)(xz + 1) \geq 8\sqrt{4x^2y^2z^2} = 16\sqrt{xyz}^2 = 16\sqrt{1}^2 = 16\]
Equality holds when all terms in the AM-GM inequalities are equal, which leads to \(x = 2y = 2z\) and \(xyz = 1\). Solving, we find \(x = 2\), \(y = 1\), and \(z = \frac{1}{2}\).
